                              PRESSURE



   The room sighed.
   “Thank you, Mrs.—” said the chair. “We’ll need documentation.”
   She stood there a moment longer. Then she sat down.
   Nobody had been cruel. That was what I could not get past. Every
person in that room had been decent, and the station had closed any‐
way, because the baby had not been a document.
   The auditorium came back. I was standing at the microphone in my
own century, holding a piece of paper.
   “My name is Juan Carlos Cabral,” I said. “I live on Jerome, between
167th and 168th.”
   I read it. Not the stones, not the loop. I read the date.
   “I have in my hand an observation report for four properties in this
district, including the café across from the Ballroom and a residential
building with eighty-one tenants. It lists conditions found unsafe. It
was prepared by Aperture Civic. It is dated June fourteenth.” I looked
up. “The conditions were observed June thirteenth.”
   A murmur.
   “That is the night of the permit you’re being asked to approve. The
report was written before the night happened. Somebody wrote the
weather before the storm.” I put the paper down. “I’m not asking you
to believe a thing about sound, or about me. I’m asking you to read the
date.”
   I stepped back.
   In the second row, Mamá sat up very straight on her folding chair.
She didn’t clap. She lifted one hand off her purse and put it down
again, which in her language is a standing ovation.



The board voted nine to four.
